## What the live box taught me

I smoke-tested the real CLI against an isolated `WAKELITE_HOME` (read-only, no `--fix`, nothing written under `~/.wakelite`), and it caught two things the tests alone would not have.

First, `cmux-focus-server` — the daemon from the incident — declares a resource called `cmux-focus-port` and records 17382 in its description, not in a form any strict parser reads. So doctor accepts three declaration forms: a resource named `port:17382`, any number inside a resource whose name mentions a port, or `--port`/`PORT=`/`127.0.0.1:NNNN` in the command. When a resource names a port but carries no readable number, the report says so by name instead of silently showing nothing.

Second, that description contains `127.0.0.1:17382`, and my first pass reported a phantom port `127` alongside the real one. The number pattern now rejects octets of a dotted quad, and the fallback test asserts that the parsed set is exactly `[17382]`.

The final live report reads correctly:

```
Runner:    healthy — heartbeat 3s old, REST API answering
Ports:
  17382 (cmux-focus-server): held by pid 37642 — ours
  8791 (<redacted>-report-server): nobody is listening
Incidents: 0 unacknowledged
No problems found — WakeLite is healthy.
```

Two notes on design worth your attention. `--fix` action 1 resolves `wakelite.service.reclaim_orphans(state)` by name at call time; R1 has not landed, so today it reports `unavailable` and the moment R1 merges under that name doctor picks it up with no further change. If R1 lands under a different name, that one line needs updating. And I added a single line to `service.py` recording `runner.pid` in `meta` once per scheduler start, so the parentage check can tell a daemon fathered by the live runner from one orphaned by an earlier one; without it doctor falls back to matching runner-ish process arguments.

**Done:** `wakelitectl doctor` with REST-then-database fallback, bounded `--fix`, `--quiet`/`--json`, 5 tests, README and CLAUDE.md updates, committed as `0abd929` · **Left:** the live verification that is yours — deploying the runner and running `doctor --fix` for real against `launchctl`

👉 Restart the runner so `runner.pid` starts being recorded, then run `PYTHONPATH=. ./bin/wakelitectl doctor` against the live home to confirm the daemon and parentage lines read correctly before you trust `--fix`.
